.SS "sprintf(), vsprintf()"
.\" .P
.\" Linux libc4.[45] does not have a
.\" .BR snprintf (),
.\" but provides a libbsd that contains an
.\" .BR snprintf ()
.\" equivalent to
.\" .BR sprintf (),
.\" that is, one that ignores the
.\" .I size
.\" argument.
.\" Thus, the use of
.\" .BR snprintf ()
.\" with early libc4 leads to serious security problems.
.\" .P
.\" Some floating-point conversions under early libc4
.\" caused memory leaks.
Because \fBsprintf\fP()  and \fBvsprintf\fP()  assume an arbitrarily long string,
callers must be careful not to overflow the actual space; this is often
impossible to assure.  Note that the length of the strings produced is
locale\-dependent and difficult to predict.  Use \fBsnprintf\fP()  and
\fBvsnprintf\fP()  instead (or \fBasprintf\fP(3)  and \fBvasprintf\fP(3)).
.SH 関連項目
\fBasprintf\fP(3), \fBsnprintf\fP(3)
